{
    "word": "systematics",
    "lang_code": "en",
    "source_file_to_entries": {
        "raw-wiktextract-data.jsonl": [
            {
                "entry_id": "91414181-1ca6-3620-8f15-83097f6fbfdd",
                "word": "systematics",
                "pos": "noun",
                "lang_code": "en",
                "forms": [],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "e57990a5-3ddc-3c08-a9f2-302bbc025668",
                        "entry_id": "91414181-1ca6-3620-8f15-83097f6fbfdd",
                        "glosses": [
                            "The study of classification systems and nomenclature."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "uncountable"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "b9ea4db8-ac86-4e39-a532-1d3f00cd7a5a",
                                "sense_id": "e57990a5-3ddc-3c08-a9f2-302bbc025668",
                                "text": "Systematics is intended to be the study of \"qualitative\" and structural aspects of all things in the universe; and if feasible, is designed to absorb eventually the \"quantitative approach\" of mathematics toward human and physical worlds as well. It is hoped that systematics will be able to develop a quantitative field and thus to enable it to incorporate mathematics into its realm of studies some time in the future."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "9fd53baf-69a1-4169-9d01-bdc953c3b2dc",
                                "sense_id": "e57990a5-3ddc-3c08-a9f2-302bbc025668",
                                "text": "Trees are the central objects of systematic analysis. Taxa are ordered, characters explained, and hypotheses tested on trees. Since systematics informs and draws on other areas of science, there is a diversity of terminology for trees and their components."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "b20679c3-1ba9-3b82-b032-6aad6fca5f2e",
                        "entry_id": "91414181-1ca6-3620-8f15-83097f6fbfdd",
                        "glosses": [
                            "The classification system of a branch of science, especially the classification of organisms in biology."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "uncountable"
                        ],
                        "examples": []
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "64fe7f12-3fd6-309a-9fe7-85bebdaa8045",
                        "entry_id": "91414181-1ca6-3620-8f15-83097f6fbfdd",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A branch of Christian theology that formulates an orderly, rational, and coherent account of Christian beliefs. It comprises dogmatics, ethics and philosophy of religion."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "uncountable"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "65a9ccfb-a6f5-4b50-872d-0cfab31a94ec",
                                "sense_id": "64fe7f12-3fd6-309a-9fe7-85bebdaa8045",
                                "text": "Systematics is an effort to understand those specifically theological affirmations that the theologian holds to be true and so regards as doctrines."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "c640452d-266a-4562-8e25-6a7c8a5e2567",
                                "sense_id": "64fe7f12-3fd6-309a-9fe7-85bebdaa8045",
                                "text": "The seventh functional specialty, systematics, is concerned with promoting an understanding of the realitites affirmed in the previous specialty, doctrines."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "976dace5-2f25-4ed1-af2a-2083a8dc16ce",
                                "sense_id": "64fe7f12-3fd6-309a-9fe7-85bebdaa8045",
                                "text": "Not exegesis itself, then, but biblical theology, provides the material for systematics .... Bliblical theology is not, then, a rival of systematics; it is not even a parallel product of the same body of facts, provided by exegesis; it is the basis and source of systematics."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "99edb9e7-9341-3933-b56d-79f4a2f54cf0",
                        "entry_id": "91414181-1ca6-3620-8f15-83097f6fbfdd",
                        "glosses": [
                            "The place where legal provisions stand as relevant for their interpretation."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "uncountable"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "c70043dd-77db-472e-bf04-57c941d90da7",
                                "sense_id": "99edb9e7-9341-3933-b56d-79f4a2f54cf0",
                                "text": "Different kinds of systematic interpretation of statutes affect each other. Construction of a statutory provision depends at the same time on interpretation of other provisions, systematics of the statute, conceptual analysis and theories formulated in legal dogmatics."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "3bd525e6-3eed-4a3e-837f-8aff9c86c678",
                        "source_entry_id": "91414181-1ca6-3620-8f15-83097f6fbfdd",
                        "source_sense_description": "the systematic classification of organisms",
                        "target_lang_word": "systematika",
                        "target_lang_code": "cs",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": []
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "a8df1734-72c2-4820-8719-693be70f8aaf",
                        "source_entry_id": "91414181-1ca6-3620-8f15-83097f6fbfdd",
                        "source_sense_description": "the systematic classification of organisms",
                        "target_lang_word": "Systematik",
                        "target_lang_code": "de",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "Systematik",
                                "target_lang_code": "de",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "19e9bdf6-fa4b-35f5-aaed-2bf4f7f550b6",
                                        "entry_id": "da13ed3c-3436-32d5-860d-afa2d70f6286",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "planmäßige, einheitliche Darstellung in Klassen, Kategorien oder anderen abstrakten Konzepten, die bestimmten Ordnungsprinzipien, einem System folgen"
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "8b25f64c-358a-474e-94b6-64d9392e880d",
                                                "sense_id": "19e9bdf6-fa4b-35f5-aaed-2bf4f7f550b6",
                                                "text": "„Innerhalb einer Systematik gibt es die Gruppen ‚Allgemeines’ und ‚Sonstiges’.“ (Internetbeleg)"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "ce0e5d78-9eb6-4706-bca6-101df6d7be48",
                                                "sense_id": "19e9bdf6-fa4b-35f5-aaed-2bf4f7f550b6",
                                                "text": "Machen Sie einen Kurs in Buchhaltung, und dann werden Sie die Systematik schnell durchschauen."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "ecf6df83-5d89-4049-a0f1-a8ea77a98b0c",
                                                "sense_id": "19e9bdf6-fa4b-35f5-aaed-2bf4f7f550b6",
                                                "text": "„Diese Systematik der Namensgebung sorgt natürlich für Unordnung.“"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "2434d2a2-e3f7-458c-8c2e-550b608e63dc",
                                                "sense_id": "19e9bdf6-fa4b-35f5-aaed-2bf4f7f550b6",
                                                "text": "„Die neuere Systematik teilt die Primaten in vier Unterordnungen: Lemuroidea oder Lemuren, Galagoidea oder Buschbabys und Loris, Tarsioidea oder Gespenstmakis und Simiae oder Affen.“"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "8a2e29a5-bb1b-4f32-b4ff-a51c86d0e82e",
                                                "sense_id": "19e9bdf6-fa4b-35f5-aaed-2bf4f7f550b6",
                                                "text": "„Nur Sade konnte diese Systematik der Psychopathia sexualis liefern.“"
                                            }
                                        ],
                                        "sense_index_json": "\"1\""
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
